# Entropy — ChaosAgent (Autonomous Site Reliability & Blast-Radius Engine)

## 1. Overview & Problem Statement
Microservices are tangled webs of dependencies. When one service slows down or crashes, it can trigger a domino effect across upstream applications. Site Reliability Engineers (SREs) struggle to forecast the **blast radius** of a failure before it happens in production.

**ChaosAgent (Entropy)** is an offensive, autonomous reliability agent that:
1. **Maps Topology (Neo4j)**: Stores microservice architecture as a directed dependency graph and uses GraphRAG to identify single points of failure and synchronous dependency bottlenecks.
2. **Generates Failure Hypotheses (Nosana)**: Queries the Neo4j graph and uses a Nosana-hosted LLM to formulate targeted chaos hypotheses (e.g., *"If RedisCache latency spikes to 3,000ms, will synchronous token verification in PaymentService exhaust its worker pool and hang the Frontend checkout flow?"*).
3. **Executes & Measures Blast Radius (Daytona)**: Spins up an isolated **Daytona** sandbox running a miniature multi-service mock cluster, injects real faults (latency spikes, packet drops, process kills), fires concurrent user traffic, and measures p50/p95/p99 latencies and cascading timeouts.
4. **Maps the Cascade & Self-Heals (Neo4j + Nosana + Daytona)**: Updates Neo4j in real time—highlighting cascaded nodes in **RED**—generates a concrete TypeScript circuit-breaker/timeout remediation patch via Nosana, hot-applies the fix inside the Daytona sandbox, and re-runs the chaos test to prove the blast radius shrinks from 5 red nodes to 1 isolated node.

